steps:

  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 8
      - mark 1 B 13
      - mark 1 C 21
    focus: [1, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;Handling One-Shot Interrupts}

      With basic {[cu]Gpio} and {[cu]ConsoleUart} services in place, we'll pivot to handling HW interrupts inside a {[cu]OneShot} driver.

      <div class="em-info">

      Managing interrupts indeed raises the bar for **EMS** dis&shy;tros in general &ndash; especially when supporting new MCUs.

      </div>

      To simplify matters, we'll examine a {[cu]OneShot} driver {[bm,A]} used throughout **Basic Examples**&thinsp; {[tr,101/04/01]} and&thinsp; {[tr,101/05/01]}.

      Note that the design of {[cu]OneShot1P} directly uses (distro-supplied) {[cn]GlobalInterrupts}{[bm,B]} and {[cn]Idle}{[bm,C]} modules.

  - cmds:
      - open 2
      - fold 2 3 7
      - fold 2 8 12
      - fold 2 13 14
      - fold 2 15 17
      - fold 2 19 21
      - fold 2 25 52
      - fold 2 53 69
      - fold 2 71 73
      - fold 2 74 83
      - fold 2 84 88
      - mark 2 A 8
      - mark 2 B 13
      - mark 2 C 15
    focus: [2, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]BoardC% Additions}

      Starting with a baseline {[cu]BoardC} viewed in the last tour {[tr,103/05/02]}, supporting {[cu]OneShot} requires minimal additions.

      Notice how the modules imported by {[cu]BoardC} now fall into _three_ categories, based on their bucket of origin:

      {[BM,A]} portable &ndash; {[cu]Common}, {[cu]Poller}<br>
      {[BM,B]} MCU-specific &ndash; {[cu]Idle}, {[cu]OneShot}<br>
      {[BM,C]} ARM-generic &ndash; {[cu]GlobalInterrupts}, {[cu]UsCounter}

      With multiple **EMS** distros supporting a range of **ARM** processors, {[cs]'@em.arch.arm'} has earned its keep.

      <div class="em-info">

      In the limit, we want to _minimize_ each distro's unique contributions and _maximize_ re-use of generic elements.

      </div>
      

  - cmds:
      - open 4
      - fold 4 18 100
      - mark 4 A 4
      - mark 4 B 14
    focus: [4, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]OneShot% Driver &ndash; <code><b>em$meta</b></code>}

      This implementation of the {[cu]OneShotI} in&shy;terface imports the {[cs]'@rpi.distro.2040'} {[cu]REGS} facade as {[cs]$R}{[bm,A]}.

      Using the {[cs]$R}.{[cn]TIMER}.{[cn]ALARM0} peripheral, {[cu]OneShotAlarm0} must manage interrupts sourced by the underlying MCU.

      <div class="em-info">

      This implementation therefore imports {[cu]IntrVec} &ndash; first seen in the context of {[cu]BuildC} {[tr,103/03/04]}.

      </div>

      Invoked at build-time&thinsp;{[bm,B]}, the {[cf]useIntr} meta-function tells {[cu]IntrVec} that we'll handle {[cs]'TIMER_IRQ_0'} interrupts.

      A name _unique_ to this MCU, let's return to {[cu]BuildC} where we'll find the original declaration of {[cs]'TIMER_IRQ_0'}&hellip;

  - cmds:
      - view 6
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]BuildC% &ndash; Interrupt Declarations}

      The {[cu]BuildC} composite of each (ARM-based) distro def&shy;ines {[cn]NVIC_INTRS} &ndash; a list of MCU-specific interrupt names.

      The {[cs]em$configure} intrinsic of each {[cu]BoardC} then invokes {[cu]IntrVec}.**`em$meta`**.{[cf]addIntr}{[dc,I]} to declare each name.

      <div class="em-info">

      Note that our first overview of {[cu]BoardC}&thinsp;{[tr,103/03/03]} had an _empty_ {[cn]NVIC_INTRS} &ndash; suitable for its `..sandbox` distro.

      </div>

      Each interrupt name in fact declares a _family_ of program identifiers &ndash; as we'll see next with {[cs]'TIMER_IRQ_0'}{[dc,T]}.


  - cmds:
      - open 4
      - mark 4 A 4
      - mark 4 B 14
      - mark 4 C 21
      - mark 4 D 26
      - mark 4 E 41
      - mark 4 F 50
    focus: [4, 23]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]OneShot% Driver &ndash; <code><b>em$targ</b></code>}

      The {[cn]IRQn} constant at&thinsp;{[bm,C]} aliases a (vendor-supplied) sym&shy;bol defining the interrupt number of {[cs]'TIMER_IRQ_0'}.

      <div class="em-info">

      Calls to {[cu]IntrVec}.{[cf]NVIC_*} inside functions {[bm,D]}&thinsp;&ndash;{[bm,F]} re&shy;ceive {[cn]IRQn} as their argument.

      </div>

      Implementations of the {[cu]OneShotI} {[cf]enable} and {[cf]uenable} methods defer to a common {[cf]ustart} function {[bm,E]}.

      Besides preparing {[cs]$R}.{[cn]TIMER}, {[cf]ustart} calls the {[cf]setLevel} method of {[cu]Idle} &ndash; which we'll explain shortly.

      To handle {[cs]'TIMER_IRQ_0'} interrupts, the driver must im&shy;plement a specially-named {[cf]*_isr$$} function {[bm,F]}.

      When triggered, {[cf]*_isr$$} clears the appropriate inter&shy;rupt sources and invokes the {[cn]cur_fxn} handler bound in {[bm,E]}.

  - cmds:
      - view 7
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;ARM Core Support}

      The `em.arch.arm` bucket contains generic run-time con&shy;tent targeting ARM-based MCUs:

      {[DC,1]} standard ARM core header files included via {[cu]REGS}<br>
      {[DC,2]} stock implementation of {[cu]GlobalInterruptsI}<br>
      {[DC,3]} implementation of {[cu]IrqI} plus a default {[cu]IsrI} proxy<br>
      {[DC,4]} implementation of {[cu]IsrI} with debug output<br>
      {[DC,5]} minimal implementation of {[cu]IsrI}<br>
      {[DC,6]} stock implementation of {[cu]UsCounterI}<br>

      Note also that {[cu]IntrVec}{[dc,M]} along with {[cu]StartupC}{[dc,M]} both play important roles in **`em$meta`** domain.

      <div class="em-info">

      With other distros targeting MCUs based on **RISC-V** or **ARC** cores, expect a similar design to emerge.

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - open 3
      - fold 3 7 22
      - fold 3 40 51
      - mark 3 A 28
      - mark 3 B 31
      - mark 3 C 40
      - mark 3 D 54
      - mark 3 E 66
    focus: [3, 25]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]IdleI% Implementation}

      An earlier **Basic Examples** tour {[tr,101/04/06]} mentioned how a distro's {[cu]Idle} module uses `DbgB` to signal MCU activity.

      Set "active"&thinsp;{[bm,A]} at startup, {[cf]doPause}{[bm,B]} signals on `DbgB` the MCU's entry into "standby" via the ARM `wfi` instruction.

      <div class="em-info">

      The internal {[cf]doSleep}{[bm,C]} function (examined on the next tour) signals entry into a "deeper" mode of MCU sleep.

      </div>

      Depending upon {[cn]cur_level}, our public {[cf]exec}{[bm,D]} imple&shy;mentation "idles" MCU execution using either {[bm,B]} or {[bm,C]}.

      Recall how our {[cu]OneShot} driver seen earlier on this tour called {[cf]setLevel}{[bm,E]} &ndash; forcing use of {[cf]doPause} when idling.

      <div class="em-info">

      The next tour explains the tradeoffs between {[bm,B]} and {[bm,C]}.

      </div>



